<pgblk chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="PGBLK-25-15-65-0001-" revdate="20250131">
<title>DESCRIPTION AND OPERATION</title>
<task chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="870" seq="801"
confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="1" chg="u"
key="TASK-25-15-65-870-801-A01" revdate="20250131">
<title>Description</title>
<topic><?Pub Dtl><title>General</title>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>This manual contains maintenance and repair instructions for
Pilot and Copilot seats type 444 manufactured by SAFRAN SEATS (SAFRAN
SEATS FRANCE).</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The pilot seat is installed left hand and copilot seat right
hand while entering in the cockpit. The two seats are composed of
the same or symmetrical parts such as the seat controls which are
located symmetrically from one seat to the other.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The two seats are installed with adjustable armrests and seat
backs, and can be adjusted for height as well as fore position.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The seats are equipped with a multi point restraint system which
is attached on the seat primary structure and the seat back.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The Equipment feature comfort design. They consist of an aluminum
base frame, fully upholstered seat back and bottom cushion assemblies.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The numerous requirements for demonstrating that a "seat system"
meets all the static and dynamic loads, the permanent deformation
limitations, and the injury criteria, imply that individual elements
of a "seat system" cannot be evaluated independently from each other.
This difficulty translates into keeping the integrity of structures
and other essential features affecting the performance of the "seat
system" intact as certified. Modifications by a non-OEM, or the replacement
of a non- OEM component, will invalidate the certification, unless
certified by the OEM.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Seat Description</title>
<grphcref refid="graphic-25-15-65-991-001-a01"></grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The pilot and copilot seats are parts of cockpit equipment (forward
fuselage). The pilot and copilot seats are symmetrical in regard of
aircraft longitudinal center line. The pilot seat is located left
hand and copilot seat right hand while entering in the cockpit. The
two seats are composed of the same or symmetrical parts such as the
seat controls which are located symmetrically from one seat to the
other.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The pilot and copilot seats consists of a lower primary structure,
a seat pan, a backrest, a headrest, armrests and a restraint system.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The lower primary structure includes seat longitudinal adjustment
device, seat lateral stowage device, seat locking device, seat height
adjustment device. All the parts involved on lower primary structure
are considered as load path structure.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The seat structure is designed to give comfort. The backrest
reclining and devices such as adjustable lumbar support, armrests
and headrest take part in pilot/copilot tiredness reduction and comfort
increase.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The seat pan consists of load path structure, cushioning, seat
adjustment controls, thigh rest system and fairing.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The backrest consists of load path structure, cushioning, tilt
system, life vest stowage, headrest system, pneumatic lumbar support
system and fairing.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The armrest system is attached to the seat pan structure.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The seat mainly use aluminum machined parts for structure, sheet
metal and pipes attached to each other by screws. Cushions are made
of polyurethane foam attached on the structure with Auto-Agrip and
fairings are made of Polycarbonate. Items that have a relative displacement
are equipped with sliding parts.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Each seat is attached on cockpit floor with two seat tracks
which let them to be adjusted fore and aft.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>A vertical seat adjustment lets the pilot/copilot to adjust
properly their eyes with regard to the cockpit environment.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>An electrical actuator system make the vertical displacements
of the seat.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The restraint system is installed on seat pan subassembly for
lap belt crotch straps, and on backrest subassembly for the shoulder
straps with inertia real. The restraint is equipped with a manual
locking device of the inertia reel. The rotary buckle is attached
in the external strap.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem></prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15"
subjnbr="65" func="991" seq="001" confltr="A" varnbr="01"
pgblknbr="0001" chg="u" key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-001-A01"
revdate="20250131"><title>Seat Description</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-001-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-001-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Lower primary structure</title>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The lower structure which has the main function to link the
seat pan to the aircraft seat tracks. It lets the seat to be adjusted
fore and aft, laterally and vertically.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The lower structure is composed of a &ldquo;fixed structure&rdquo;
that links the seat to the aircraft and a &ldquo;moving structure&rdquo;
linked with seat pan and backrest.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The &ldquo;fixed structure&rdquo; is composed of two aluminum
machined frames linked each other with transversal tubes and rear
aluminum sheet metal. Two transversal tubes are used to attach this
structure to the two longitudinal seat tracks through longitudinal
guiding device and longitudinal locking device. This structure supports
also the &ldquo;moving structure&rdquo; through vertical guiding device
and vertical locking device. The &ldquo;moving structure&rdquo; is
composed of two aluminum machined frames linked with seat pan and
backrest.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Adjustment</para>
</prcitem>
<prclist2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<para>Vertical adjustment</para>
</prcitem>
<prclist3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>The seat is attached between &ldquo;fixed structure&rdquo; and
&ldquo;moving structure&rdquo;. It is adjusted in height with a range
of 185 mm (7.28 in). This movement is supplied by an electrical linear
actuator allowing an infinite setting between two end stops. The locking
device is insured with the irreversibility of the actuator.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>Max static &amp; dynamic loads at actuator interfaces are (for
a 95%) maximum eligible: </para>
</prcitem>
<prclist4>
<prcitem4><prcitem>
<unlist bulltype="ndash"><unlitem><para>3750 daN (8430.34 Lbf) in
tension (no loss of performance),</para>
</unlitem>
<unlitem><para>2500 daN (5620.22 Lbf) in compression and extended
position (no loss of performance).</para>
</unlitem>
</unlist>
</prcitem></prcitem4>
</prclist4>
</prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>The shaft actuator moves on 100 mm (3.94 in), the actuator on
is pivot link give a transverse and angular movement.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>The height adjustment induce a fore or aft displacement of the
seat pan.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>Mechanical back-up:</para>
</prcitem>
<prclist4>
<prcitem4><prcitem>
<para>The back-up is done by mechanical crank located in area sup
of the actuator.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem4>
<prcitem4><prcitem></prcitem></prcitem4>
</prclist4>
</prcitem3>
</prclist3>
</prcitem2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<para>Longitudinal adjustment</para>
</prcitem>
<prclist3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>For the longitudinal adjustment, the seat is installed on a
seat track defined by Dassault and Safran Seats France (for interfaces).</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>Seat longitudinal adjustment device is supplied by the use of
rollers equipped of sleeves installed on lower seat base. These rollers
let seat to move longitudinally onto tracks (on the front slider system).
Flanges, receiving the rollers, have lips to get caught on seat tracks;
onto the rear slider system, plastic sliders participates in the guide.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>This adjustment is controlled with a lever located on right
hand side of seat pan. The total stroke is 247 mm (9.72 in) adjustable
by increment of 19 mm (0.75 in).</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>The locking in position is got with two pins going inside a
cut out machined in lateral inboard lip of each seat tracks. Locking
device is located at the rear of seat base. In each position the seat
is attached by two lock pins located on the rear seat base.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>The locking must be honest and sure (no risk that the pilot
believed lock when he is not) The sliding force of the seat on the
seat track is 15 daN (33.72 Lbf).</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>Longitudinal displacement:</para>
</prcitem>
<prclist4>
<prcitem4><prcitem>
<para>The Longitudinal displacement is ensured with aluminium rollers
which are attached on the crosspieces by screws. These rollers lets
seat to move longitudinally onto tracks and ensure the longitudinal
guidance by the two inboard side rollers which have reduced gap with
seat tracks (gap is 0.3 mm (0.01 in)).</para>
</prcitem></prcitem4>
<prcitem4><prcitem>
<para>Locking longitudinal: The longitudinal locking system is located
on the rear of lower structure. The longitudinal locking control is
insured by forks and levers. The longitudinal locking pin (phi 7.5
mm (0.3 in)) follows standard Safran Seats France conical rounded
shape and the locking pin loading spring is rated at 30 N (6.74 Lbf)
(per spring) maximum in order to limit seat track wear, minimum loading
spring is 20 N (4.50 Lbf).</para>
</prcitem></prcitem4>
</prclist4>
</prcitem3>
</prclist3>
</prcitem2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<para>Lateral adjustment</para>
</prcitem>
<prclist3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>In order to help access, the seat is equipped with 100 mm lateral
movement outboard. The lateral locking is equivalent as the longitudinal
locking. The maximum force necessary to displace laterally the seat
while 104 kg loaded over seat pan is 3 daN (6.74 Lbf).</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>Lateral adjustment is ensured with four rollers. those rollers
are attached on the crosspieces with rollers axis. Each roller axis
is composed of a nut, a screw and a spacer.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
<prcitem3><prcitem>
<para>The lateral adjustment shall only be done if the seat is in
the maximum rear position.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem3>
</prclist3>
</prcitem2>
</prclist2>
</prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Seat pan</title>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Seat pan load path structure is a rigid metallic structure that
supports mainly the occupant weight and all the associated load factors.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The seat pan load path structure has two machined aluminium
spreaders linked to each other by steel front and rear seat pan crosspieces.
An aluminium sheet metal is riveted to structure.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Two other machined aluminium link spreaders and machined aluminium
part make the interfaces with the electrical actuator.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The seat pan structure also supports the arm of the armrest.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>In the front of the seat pan is the thighrest which is adjustable
by the mean of a button located onto the inboard seat pan.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The seat pan supports most of seat adjustment controls to improve
human interface.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The seat is attached to the lower structure via the connecting
rods.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Seat pan cushioning which participate greatly in pilot comfort
comprise polyurethane foam (fire blocking foam) and fabric cover attached
onto foam with Auto-Agrip.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The cushion is attached by Auto-Agrip on the seat pan. The pressure
mapping study has permitted to increase comfort by increasing pressure
distribution and preventing local high pressure which lead to discomfort.
The shape of the cushion prevent that the pilot is sliding forward.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Thigh rest</para>
</prcitem>
<prclist2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<para>Thigh rest setting is between -7&deg; mini and 31&deg; mini
with all intermediate positions achievable.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<para>Load to apply on thigh rest to reduce thigh pressure, while
pushing the button, is 15 daN (33.72 Lbf).</para>
</prcitem></prcitem2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<para>The activation of the thigh rest is made by a cylinder located
below the seat pan and attached to the inboard seat pan machined part.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<para>The cylinder is controlled by a button and a cable.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<para>The maxi (32&deg;) and the mini (-8&deg;) position are limited
by mechanical end stops.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<para>The design of the gas spring is that it can be carried out at
temperatures of from ambient 80&deg;C to -30&deg;C (176&deg;F to -22&deg;F).
Past these temperatures the cylinder remains in position.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem2>
</prclist2>
</prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Backrest</title>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Backrest load path structure is a tubular structure composed
of one tube, reinforced locally with sleeves, linked each other with
mounting flanges. It is designed to deform within the limit in order
to absorb energy and to limit load on the lower part of the seat.
Also tilt mechanism participate in the backrest retention.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The backrest load path structure has: the aluminum tubes linked
on two mounting flanges allowing backrest rotation for tilt and backrest
forward end stop, 2 mounting flanges in the middle and the 2 last
mounting flanges at headrest level.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Two middle mounting flanges is used to attach the two tilt hydrolocks
in on hand. An aluminum metal sheet is attached on the front face
of the backrest structure to let backrest cushioning and fairing fixation,
a second metal sheet is attached at the rear of the backrest structure
to attach the safety belt retractor and a additional teflon is used
to ensure a good slide strap.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Tilt system is insured by several tilt position locks mounted
at the bottom of the backrest. Two hydrolocks for tilt are made of
machined steel parts and aluminum housing equipped with rings that
make chambers into which circulate oil for hydraulic locking. A pin
located at its extremity controls hydrolock. Those hydrolocks are
part of the load path and stands up to maximum of 1700 daN (3821.75
Lbf).</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The backrest is attached to the seat pan structure by mean of
two screws at articulation level and four screws at hydrolock fixation.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The backrest foam is attached by Auto-Agrip over aluminum metal
sheet. The backrest cover, that envelops foam and lumbar support,
is attached by Auto-Agrip over the backrest structure.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>For the comfort of the cushion, forms are &ldquo;bucket-type
effect&rdquo; which also supplies lateral support. The backrest cushion
is made to integrate a pneumatic lumbar support.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Backrest tilt system</title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-002-A01"> </grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Backrest tilt system comprise two tilt hydrolocks (10) attached
by screw and spacer to the transversal backrest tube in one hand and
on aft seat pan crosspiece hinge fitting in the other hand, a tilt
command composed of a tilt lever (20) located at seat pan level inboard,
a tilt hydrolock connector (30) per each tilt hydrolock, and a control
cable (40) that link the lever to the two tilt hydrolock connectors.
The system kinematics let 32&deg; tilt from 8&deg; upright position.
Each tilt hydrolock (10) has a load path structure, an adjustment
control system, a hydraulic locking system, a spring for backrest
raising and an override system to tilt manually the backrest to upright
position in case of controls or tilt hydrolock failure.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Apply a force against the back cushion until correct tilt is
got and then release the button. The backrest can be returned to the
upright position by removing all force from back and pulling the reclining
control handle.</para>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="002" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-002-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Backrest
Tilt System</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-002-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-002-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Lumbar support system</title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-003-A01"> </grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The pneumatic lumbar is made to improve the comfort of the pilot.
Lumbar adjustment is done by pressing the button and the force applied
by the pilot on the lumbar.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The lumbar support is attached to the backrest sheet with Auto-Agrip
tape (hook on bucket / loop on lumbar support). The bucket is made
of composite materials and includes ergonomic shape for the bottom
and the upper backrest, and receives the backrest cushion made of
polyurethane foam.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>There is no vertical adjustment: there is no need for a vertical
adjustment of the lumbar support as the shape of the lumbar support
is, by design, adaptable to the position of the lumbar hole position
of the occupant. Thus the shape of the cushion adapts refer the positioning
/ lumbar pilot when he adjust inflate or deflate this one.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Lumbar support system comprise an inflatable pouch (10) made
of polyester urethane attached by Auto-Agrip over backrest metal sheet,
a vent valve (15) let pouch inflation setting, a push button (30)
located at seat pan level outboard, and a control cable (40) that
link push button to pouch valve.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The lumbar support control device is located on the outboard
side of the seat pan. Push button is pressed to open the valve and
let the air contained in the pouch to get out while pilot is pushing
with is back to get necessary setting, then push button has to be
released to lock the setting. To inflate the pouch, the push button
has to be pressed while pilot is not in contact with backrest. Lumbar
support inflation is allowed by the foam in the pouch that is used
as a spring.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The lumbar support has two valves installed, which control the
volume inside the lumbar support inflatable pouch. The vent valve
(15) is a bidirectional valve and it is only actuated when the push
button is pressed, allowing the lumbar support volume to be adjusted
when pressure is applied over the seat backrest. The outlet valve
(20) is an unidirectional valve and is intended to eliminate any significant
variation of volume inside the lumbar support inflatable pouch during
aircraft take-off/climb, equalizing the pouch internal pressure with
cabin pressure as cabin altitude increases. As a result of this two
valve design, the lumbar support P/N  F0502828 inflatable pouch volume
does not present significant volume variation during take-off/climb.</para>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="003" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-003-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Lumbar
Support System</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-003-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-003-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Life jacket box and crank handle stowage</title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-004-A01"> </grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The life jacket stowage box is located below the seat pan.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The box is attached by four screws on the crossover of the seat
pan.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The life jacket is maintained in the box by snap strap.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The life jacket stowage box is composed of: A lower cover thermoformed,
four straps and a sheet metal part. Lower cover is attached on sheet
metal by eight pop. The closure straps are popped on the lower thermoformed
cover and the sheet metal and linked together by snap. </para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The stowage of the crank handle P/N 139010 consist of a recess
created on the aft of lower structure fairing on the pilot seat.</para>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="004" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-004-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Life
Vest Stowage</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-004-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-004-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Headrest system</title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-005-A01"></grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Headrest system positioned on the top of the backrest includes
a structure (10) composed of a polycarbonate shell allowing the rotational
movement, a vertical adjustment system (20) composed of two nylon
rod guides for height adjustment setting,  and two notch rods attached
to headrest structure,  cushion and fabric cover (30) attached onto
foam with Auto-Agrip. This cushion is attach by Auto-Agrip into structure
shell.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Headrest is attached by two accessible screws in the vertical
adjustment system (10).</para>
<?Pub _newline>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="005" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-005-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Headrest
System</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-005-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-005-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Armrest system</title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-006-A01"> </grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The armrest structure is composed by a structure (10) made of
a machined aluminium hinge (20), folded and spot welded aluminium
sheets metal. It has also finishing parts such has lower fairing made
of polycarbonate and an armcap made of a self skinning foam attached
to the movable upper structure. </para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The rotation around Y axis lets the vertical adjustment (30)
of the armrest. A knob at the end of the armrest lets this adjustment.
This way, the angular position of the armrest could be adjusted continuously
between -15&deg; and +15&deg; taking the neutral position (horizontal).</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>This armrest includes also finishing part such has lower fairing
(40) made of polycarbonate, and an armcap (50) made of a self skinning
attached by quick release fastener to the structure.</para>
<?Pub _newline>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="006" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-006-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Armrest
System</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-006-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-006-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Fairing</title>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>All fairings are made of polycarbonate and attached to the structure
by screws.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic></task>
<task chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="870" seq="802"
confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="1" chg="u"
key="TASK-25-15-65-870-802-A01" revdate="20250131">
<title>Operation</title>
<topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Adjustment of the seat height </title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-007-A01"></grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The box structure is hinged to the seat pan at one end and hinged
with the front crosspiece to the other end.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The arms are hinged to the rear of the rearward crosspiece and
also to the middle of the sides of the box structure. Their free ends
slide upon rollers running within slots in the side frames of the
seat pan.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The electrical hydrolock is operated with the control switch.
Extension or retraction of the hydrolock rod connected to the front
crosspiece results in a change in the angle between the box structure
and the arms, thus lowering or raising the seat.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>A crank handle, stowed in the lower structure fairing of the
pilot seat, can be used to adjust pilot and copilot seat in the event
of a failure of the electrical hydrolock or of the power supply.</para>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="007" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-007-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Adjustment
Of The Seat Height</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-007-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-007-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Seat unlocking lever</title>
<grphcref refid="graphic-25-15-65-991-008-a01"></grphcref>
<caution><para>THE LATERAL MOVEMENT IS ONLY POSSIBLE WHEN THE SEAT
IS IN ITS MOST AFTWARD POSITION. TRYING TO MOVE THE SEAT LATERALLY
WHILE THE SEAT IS NOT IN ITS MOST AFTWARD POSITION can CAUSE DAMAGE
TO THE SEAT.</para>
</caution>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The seat unlocking lever has 3 positions</para>
</prcitem>
<prclist2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<unlist bulltype="ndash"><unlitem><para>Neutral position, locks the
seat,</para>
</unlitem>
<unlitem><para>Up position, unlocking lateral locking pins, lets seat
adjustment fore and aft along seat tracks between stops,</para>
</unlitem>
<unlitem><para>Down position, unlocking longitudinal locking pins,
lets seat outboard stowage when seat is at rear stop level.</para>
</unlitem>
</unlist>
</prcitem></prcitem2>
</prclist2>
<graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991" seq="008"
confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-008-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Seat
Unlocking Lever</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-008-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-008-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Longitudinal locking pin control</title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-009-A01"> </grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>An override mechanical control is available for both longitudinal
and lateral movement in case of failure of wiring. Pull the control
pin upwards to unlock the seat.</para>
<?Pub _newline>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="009" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-009-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Longitudinal
Locking Pin Control</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-009-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-009-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Lateral locking pin</title>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Operation of the lateral unlocking lever pulls the lateral cables,
which pull both locking pins; the seat is then free to move laterally.
Each locking pin is directly controlled by cable and equipped with
an override manual control.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>When the lateral unlocking lever is released, both locking pins
engage in holes in the track through the action of their return springs;
the seat is then locked in position.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Adjustment of armrest </title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-010-A01"></grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The armrests are hinged to the seat pan via arm of armrest.
They can move up and down. A longitudinal control located at the front
of the armrest lets to install the armrest cover either fully aft
or fully deployed.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The rotation around Y axis lets the vertical adjustment (&plusmn;15&deg;)
of the armrest. A knob at the end of the armrest lets this adjustment.</para><?Pub
_newline>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="010" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-010-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Armrest
Upward Adjustment</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-010-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-010-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic>
<?Pub _newpage>
<topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Backrest tilt adjustment</title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-011-A01"></grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The backrest level is the same that is used for longitudinal
and lateral movement. Moving up the backrest lever opens the hydraulic
circuit of the two hydrolocks, allowing the seat occupant to select
the necessary inclination by pressure exerted upon the backrest.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The lever in neutral position locks the backrest in position.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>The backrest return in the neutral position (8&deg;/ vertical)
with an action on the lever.</para>
<?Pub _newline>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="011" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-011-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Backrest
Tilt Adjustment</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-011-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-011-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic>
<?Pub _newpage>
<topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Cable end adjustment </title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-012-A01"></grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>When the seat occupant releases the lever control, the circuit
is closed, thus clamping the hydrolock in position and retaining the
backrest in the selected position. The screw can be adjusted to have
a correct contact with the tilt lock.</para>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="012" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-012-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Cable
End Adjustment</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-012-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-012-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic>
<?Pub _newpage>
<topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Adjustment of the thigh system</title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-013-A01"></grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para> Use the thigh rest adjustment control button to search the
correct position.</para>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="013" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-013-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Adjustment
of the Thigh System</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-013-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-013-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic><topic><?Pub Dtl><title>Adjustment of the headrest</title>
<grphcref refid="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-014-A01"></grphcref>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Move the headrest up &amp; down in order to search the correct
position.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Move the lateral ears of headrest to search the correct position.</para>
</prcitem></prcitem1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<para>Rotate the headrest assembly to search the correct position.</para>
</prcitem><graphic chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="65" func="991"
seq="014" confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" chg="u"
key="GRAPHIC-25-15-65-991-014-A01" revdate="20250131"><title>Adjustment
of the Headrest</title>
<sheet gnbr="graphic25-15-65-991-014-A01-01" sheetnbr="1" imgarea="bp"
chg="u" key="graphic25-15-65-991-014-A01-01" revdate="20250131"></sheet>
</graphic></prcitem1>
</prclist1>
</topic></task>
<task chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="63" func="870" seq="803"
confltr="A" varnbr="01" pgblknbr="0001" confnbr="1" chg="u"
key="Task-25-15-63-870-803-A01" revdate="20250131">
<title>Seat Weight</title>
<topic><?Pub Dtl>
<prclist1>
<prcitem1><prcitem>
<unlist bulltype="none">
<unlitem><para><refint reftype="1" refid="Table-25-15-63-997-003-A01">Table
25-15-63-997-003-A01</refint> gives the weight of the Pilot &amp;
Copilot Seats.</para>
</unlitem>
<unlitem><para>All weights listed below include seat covers and seat
cushions.</para>
</unlitem>
</unlist>
</prcitem>
<prclist2>
<prcitem2><prcitem>
<table chapnbr="25" sectnbr="15" subjnbr="63" func="997" seq="003"
varnbr="1" pgblknbr="0001" confnbr="1" orient="land"
id="Table-25-15-63-997-003-A01">
<title>Seat Weights</title>
<tgroup cols="3"><colspec colname="col1"><colspec colname="col2">
<colspec colname="col3">
